choice(""Maybe there are no objectives."", Ch4-Intro2, v_current_scene = Ch4-Intro2, v_checkpoint_rich = 0, v_ch4_objectives = 1)
choice(""Maybe their scrying failed and they're too proud to admit the ceremony is late."", Ch4-Intro2, v_current_scene = Ch4-Intro2, v_checkpoint_rich = 0, v_ch4_objectives = 2)
choice(""Maybe they're waiting for our numbers to decrease."", Ch4-Intro2, v_current_scene = Ch4-Intro2, v_checkpoint_rich = 0, v_ch4_objectives = 3)


ID: Ch4-Intro2
TEXT:

#if(v_ch4_objectives == 1) {
"Maybe there are no objectives," I say. "Maybe this is a war of attrition, and they're simply waiting to see who is left standing when the dust settles."

"By the gods, Barry—don't even joke about such butcher's work. If that were true, this contest becomes the largest bloodbath of the century. I refuse to believe the rulers of one of the greatest civilizations in our world would stoop to such barbarity."

A faint shift in the air cuts our conversation short. An aura is moving toward the clearing—Kate's warding barrier must have dissolved.

"Keep your guard up, Barry!" Daren's hand drops to the hilt of his broadsword.

As the newcomer draws closer, his magical signature resolves. It's faint—thin and barely perceptible, no stronger than the crossbowmen from the ambush. A straggler looking to avenge fallen comrades?

When he clears the brush, nothing about him belongs on a battlefield.

He looks nineteen at most, slender and unarmored, dressed in a loose tunic and trousers that look more like sleepwear than travel gear. His hands rest in his pockets, and his eyes are completely shut. He strolls across the uneven rock with the unhurried gait of someone wandering a quiet courtyard at dawn. Not a contestant, surely—yet he cannot be a local either, or the tournament sigils would have flared with a warning the moment he approached our perimeter.

"Good morrow, lad!" Daren calls out, stepping forward. "Fine day for a stroll, isn't it? What business brings you to our camp? If you're lost, we can point you back toward the roads."

With Daren, sincerity and sarcasm sound entirely identical—the same booming, theatrical baritone delivers both without a flicker of expression.

The boy doesn't bristle. A polite, placid smile spreads across his face, his eyes remaining shut.

"No need," the boy answers mildly. "I was in the area looking for some old acquaintances when I sensed a fellow stillwater nearby. Naturally, I had to stop by and say hello. There are so few of us left these days—it would be a shame not to greet each other in passing. My name is Eiden. Pleased to meet you."

My chest goes cold. An actual stillwater? Here?

No. Pull yourself together, Barry. It's another fraud pulling the exact same con I am. I refuse to fall for my own trick.

When I don't answer, Daren fills the silence with an easy grin.

"Pay him no mind! Barry is a bit reserved with strangers. I'm sure he's just as delighted. My name is Daren."

"Oh? Then you must be the healer in armor people have been whispering about. And it seems you have a third companion resting in your cave. A survivor of the Beacon of Hope, no less. I did not know any of them were still alive."

}
