choice(""What's the Beacon of Hope?"", Ch4-Beacon, v_current_scene = Ch4-Beacon)
choice(""Why is she a survivor? Did the Beacon of Hope burn down?"", Ch4-Survivor, v_current_scene = Ch4-Survivor)


ID: Ch4-Beacon
TEXT:

"What's the Beacon of Hope?"

"You mean she hasn't told you?" Eiden chuckles softly. "Well, far be it from me to spoil the tale. Why not ask her yourself once she wakes, my stillwater friend?"

"Let's cut the theater right there, Eiden," Daren interrupts, taking a half-step between us. "There is no need to keep up this charade. We both know Barry is an ordinary human and not a stillwater."

"Oh? You confessed?" Eiden turns toward me, eyebrow raising slightly behind closed lids. "I honestly assumed you'd ride the bluff to the bitter end. I must say, I'm impressed."

"The stillwater act won't carry you very far either, lad," Daren says, crossing his arms over his breastplate. "Particularly when you're blind. Who has ever heard of a blind stillwater?"

"Blind? What gives you that impression?"

"Your eyes have been shut since you stepped out of the brush."

"Ah. That." Eiden taps a finger against his cheek. "My vision is quite intact, actually. I shut my eyes centuries ago, simply because I grew exhausted of looking at the ugliness of humanity. Nowadays, I only bother opening them in completely deserted wilderness. My magical sense serves far better than eyesight ever did. I feel the exact weight and contour of every living creature and stone around me, with the delightful side effect of complete immunity to illusions. Why burden myself with an extra sense just to be reminded of why I despise humans so much?"

"Aren't you a bit young for existential disgust?" Daren's voice carries a dry edge. "Or is this some dramatic phase to get your parents' attention?"

Eiden smiles.

"You of all people should know better, healer in armor. You've wandered the eastern continent for decades, wading through every flavor of misery, treachery, and despair. You have seen it all, again and again. Tell me, healer—after a lifetime spent patching up the atrocities men commit against each other, do you truly believe the world is any better for your trouble?"

"The presence of evil doesn't mean the good isn't worth defending," Daren replies without hesitation. "So long as I don't give up, I will find a way."

Eiden's smile stretches wider, but his eyelids don't flinch.

"Oh? So you're an idealist, then. But tell me, hero—what happens when your pure ideals clash with the cold, hard truth of reality?"

Without warning, Daren crashes chest-first into the dirt. A terrible, crushing pressure bears down on him, grinding his plate armor into the solid rock.

"What happens when you meet a force so absolute that it simply squashes you against the ground, as if you were an insect?"

The downward force doubles with a sickening groan of fracturing stone. A deep, armored depression caves into the bedrock beneath Daren. The sheer agony tears a raw scream from his throat.

My hands go numb and cold. A violent tremor runs through my limbs. Pull yourself together, Barry. If you lose your head now, we're all dead.

"Let him go!" I yell, forcing every shred of conviction into my voice.

"You want to save your friend, Barry? Then how about we make an even trade? Your life for his. Pledge your life, and I promise to leave your friend whole. What do you say?"

choice("Accept the trade", Ch4-Accept, v_current_scene = Ch4-Accept)
choice("Propose a different trade", Ch4-Propose, v_current_scene = Ch4-Propose)


ID: Ch4-Survivor
TEXT:

"Why is she a survivor? Did the Beacon of Hope burn down?"

"Burn down? Well, I imagine the ashes scattered centuries ago. But that isn't why she's a survivor. Still, I wouldn't dream of spoiling the mystery. Why not ask her yourself once she wakes, my stillwater friend?"

"Let's cut the theater right there, Eiden," Daren interrupts, taking a half-step between us. "There is no need to keep up this charade. We both know Barry is an ordinary human and not a stillwater."

